activities.description:
The main objectives of this International Conference were defined as:
1)	Introduce participants to the technical and practical impacts of climate change-enhanced hydro-climatic hazards, with a particular emphasis on droughts and floods, through a presentation of novel methodologies applied in worldwide case studies;
2)	Highlight the critical role of citizen science and open science for effective adaptation to the impacts of those hazards;
3)	Bridging the gap between the scientific community and decision-makers from different levels in climate-risk management issues;
4)	Provide the necessary tools to leverage institutional capacity needed to support adaptation and resilience building;
5)	Follow up on the discussions of the UN Water Conference, to be held in March 2023, regarding hydro-climatic resilience; 
6)	and contribute to implementing the Strategic Plan of the 9th Phase of IHP and UNESCO Open Science Recommendation.

activities.outcomes:
•	Understanding the impact of climate change-enhanced extreme events, such as floods and drought and the vulnerability of communities;
•	Presenting state-of-the-art and innovative tools for flood and drought monitoring and early-warning systems;
•	Showcasing novel approaches to address climate change impacts, floods, droughts, adaptation and resilience-building frameworks;
•	Presenting worldwide case studies of the integration of citizen science and open science as tools to support disaster risk reduction and facilitate adaptation actions;
•	Identifying knowledge gaps that hinder the development of climate-resilient water management policies;
•	Providing space for discussion between the scientific community and decision-makers on the challenges of implementing and scaling up solutions
•	Defining feasible funding mechanisms and the broader role of climate finance to support disaster risk reduction and adaptation policies, particularly on drought and floods;
•	Continuing the discussions of the UN  2023 Water Conference by providing a space for high-level policy-oriented dialogues from key water and governmental representatives.

activities.description:
As a result of project Dynamic resilience as a measure for risk assessment of the complex water, infrastructure and ecological system: Making a context (DyRes_System), funded by the Science fund of the Republic of Serbia (PROMIS call: DyRes_System) WSDAC centre promotes final results and co-organized preparation of the Guidelines for Dynamic Resilience Assessment, as an open-access publication.The brochure aiming to enhance the predictive power in resilience assessment of the complex water system taking into account the hydroenergetic, flood, and environmental system resilience.
Guidelines for dynamic resilience assessment include several project and dissemination activities given as follows:
• Introduction;
• Dissemination activities;
• Main goals;
• General methodological framework for the dynamic resilience assessment;
• Dynamic Modeling of the Dam and Reservoir System Reduced Functionality in Adverse Operating Conditions;
• Hydroenergetic and Food Dynamic Resilience for Complex Reservoir Systems Using Failure Simulations;
• Failure Assessment of Embankment Dam Elements;
• Assessing Dynamic Resilience Under Hazardous Events using Digital twin of the water system, Artificial Neural Networks, and Data generated;
• Explainable genetic fuzzy rule-based system for resilience assessment;
• Environmental resilience under climate change;
• Conclusions;
• Acknowledgement;
• Project team members;

A live event was organized by the WSDAC Centre 2 category via the Microsoft Teams application. The final meeting was divided into two parts. First, a live event was organized to enable participants to follow presentations of the project members and guest speakers. Next, an open discussion among the team members, and participants took place. To ensure a wide communication of the research activities, participants from different sectors (energy sector academia, students, young researchers etc.) were invited. The agenda of the final meeting was given as follows:
Presentation of project results (12:00 – 13:30):
dr. Brankica Majkić-Dursun: Presentation of the WSDAC Unesco Center activities;
dr. Milan Stojković: Dynamic resilience of water resources systems: introduction, goals and project team;
Assistant prof. Dr. Damjan Ivetić: Dynamic modeling of water management systems in unfavorable operating conditions;
dr. Milan Stojković: Hydropower and flood resilience: an example of the “Pirot” water system;
Associate prof. dr. Dragan Rakić: Filtration and stress-deformation analysis of an embankment dam using the finite element method;
Assistant prof. dr. Višnja Simić: Genetically-based phase system for evaluating dynamic resilience: an example of the “Pirot” water management system;
dr. Milan Stojković: Assessment of dynamic resilience using generated data and machine learning techniques;
dr. Milan Stojković: Ecological resilience: an example of an integral water system.
	Discussion of the project team and meeting participants (13:30-14:00)

activities.outcomes:
The Centre’s scientific interests include the entire range of problems in mountainous areas, including the problem of water security, which is closely related to food and environmental security, sustainable development, social and regional stability.
           Research results:
- -glaciers of the Balkash-Alakol basin for the period from 1955/56 to 2022 decreased linearly with an average rate of -0.73-0.75% a-1 in area and about 1% a-1 in ice volume. The glaciers of the transboundary basins of the Ertis, Syrdarya and Amu Darya, as well as the mountains of High Asia as a whole, were also decreasing linearly (1955–early 1960s);
- average flow characteristics at the outflow of rivers from the mountains are stable. This stability is ensured:
- relative stability of precipitation amounts in the runoff formation zone since the middle of the 18th century;
- recirculating precipitation (more than 50% of the annual amount) due to intraregional moisture circulation;
- participation in the runoff of meltwater from ground ice under climate warming conditions (compensatory flow);
- The influent part of the water balance of the region's river runoff formation zone is relatively stable throughout the entire observation period;
The growing water shortage in the region is due to increased water intake and ineffective water resource management.

activities.notes:
Many ancestral hydrotechnologies have the full potential to be recovered and/or scaled up to contribute to the transformative change needed for responding to the global challenges in the wide framework of sustainable development. The conference established a report on main conclusions and commit to establish a Community of Practice (CoP) composed of experts from different research centres, including the UNESCO Water Family, that will collaborate on research, development and implementation of ancestral hydrotechnologies, also in liaison with the IHP´s flagship initiative WAMUNET, through its inventory of best practices and knowledge dissemination mechanisms. The CoP will focus on the following activities:
• Organize regular international or multi-regional scientific events for the sharing of knowledge and of best practices on ancestral hydrotechnologies;
• Document by scientific means, through inventories, best practices publications, journal special issues and other means, the occurrence and value of ancestral hydrotechnologies, including the associated knowledge systems and the specific sites where they occur, as a tool for sustainable development, highlighting their characteristic feature as Nature-based Solutions and their contribution to circular green-economy;
• Consider the development of an international initiative, as an independent initiative or as a component of existing ones, focusing on ancestral hydrotechnologies. It is suggested to establish an International Day on Ancestral Hydrotechnologies;
• Explore possibilities for the development of project proposals and for their implementation in the Mediterranean, Latin America and the Caribbean and in other regions for the safeguarding, recuperation, promotion or development of ancestral hydrotechnologies as a response to current emergencies.

