activities.description:
The workshop demonstrated how states can promote Ecohydrology in action in UNESCO designated sites, as well as strengthen capacity of water experts in West Africa on implementing integrated, holistic and Ecohydrology approaches for improved water management. The workshop wwas expected to support improved water management and further enhance the network of demonstration sites and basins. 
The workshop was specifically designed to achieve these objectives:- 
i.	Training up to 20 water experts including Biosphere reserve (BR)  managers to support,  BRs and designated sites in implementing  integrated, holistic and eco-hydrology approaches for improved water management;
ii.	Expose participants to guidelines/criteria for designation of Ecohydrology demonstration site; as well as share relevant experience in Africa; 
iii.	Create a platform to support the possible designation of  2 Ecohydrology  designated  sites (Jabi Lake and Lake Bosomtwe) to implement integrated, holistic and eco-hydrology approaches for improved water management;
iv.	Discuss and produced a working document on way forward for the West African Sub region- with respect to the subject of the training workshop.

activities.outcomes:
